\abstract{\small Thyroid Nodule Segmentation, Ultrasound, DeepLabV3+, EfficientNet-B5, Conformal Prediction. 
\normalsize
[17pt]
{\bf Abstract} Thyroid nodules affect a large proportion of the general population. 
Although most are benign, a non-negligible fraction is malignant. 
Ultrasound imaging is the primary diagnostic modality, and accurate nodule segmentation is a prerequisite for CAD systems to classify malignancy and assess metastatic risk.
In this work, we propose a thyroid nodule segmentation framework that integrates a DeepLabV3+, channel-attention modules and post-hoc conformal prediction, providing both accurate mask and pixel-level uncertainty estimates.
We address thyroid ultrasound nodule segmentation as the first stage of a pipeline that also includes malignancy classification and metastatic risk prediction.
We adopt DeepLabV3+, whose Atrous Spatial Pyramid Pooling (ASPP) captures multi-scale context, with an EfficientNet-B5 backbone for its accuracy-efficiency trade-off. 
Additionally, we test three channel-attention modules: Squeeze-and-Excitation, CBAM and Grouped Channel Attention (GCA), paired with two loss formulations (Dice-BCE and Boundary).
Since binary masks do not indicate prediction confidence, we wrap the trained model with a split conformal prediction layer that produces pixel-level uncertainty maps with statistical coverage guarantees.
We evaluate all configurations on the TN3K public benchmark using a fixed split and directly compare them with state-of-the-art methods. 
The plain backbone already improves upon previously reported results, with CBAM + Dice-BCE emerging as the best overall configuration.
}
