\section{Introduction}
Thyroid cancer is the most common endocrine malignancy, accounting for about $3.4\
The primary diagnostic modality is ultrasound; however, image interpretation involves a subjective component that introduces both inter- and intra-observer variability.
Computer-Aided Diagnosis (CAD) systems can reduce this variability and provide clinicians with a second opinion. 
The first stage of any thyroid cancer CAD pipeline is nodule segmentation: an accurate mask is the starting point for downstream malignancy classification and metastatic-risk quantification.
In this work, we propose a reliable nodule segmentation baseline.
We adopt DeepLabV3+~\cite{deeplab} with an EfficientNet-B5~\cite{efficientnet} backbone pre-trained on ImageNet, and we explore the performance of three channel-attention modules: Squeeze-and-Excitation (SE)~\cite{se}, Convolutional Block Attention Module (CBAM)~\cite{cbam} and Grouped Channel Attention (GCA)~\cite{gcaseg}; combined with two loss functions.
The GCA module has previously been shown to be effective in a multimodal CLIP-based framework for thyroid segmentation~\cite{gcaseg}, but here we evaluate it in a purely visual setting.
Finally, we add a conformal prediction layer~\cite{cp} to the trained model that produces pixel-level uncertainty maps with coverage guarantees.
\section{Method}
\subsection{Datasets and Preprocessing}
We use TN3K~\cite{tn3k}, a well-known open-access dataset of $3493$ thyroid ultrasound images with pixel-level nodule masks, collected from multiple medical centers and devices.
TN3K provides a fixed train-test split, which enables a direct comparison with state-of-the-art results~\cite{prochazka}; we further divide its training set into training and validation ($85\
Training images are resized to $256\times 256$ and augmented with the Albumentations library: horizontal flipping ($p=0.5$); random brightness and contrast adjustment, both up to $\pm 20\
All images are normalized using ImageNet mean and standard deviation. Validation and test images are only resized and normalized.
\subsection{Architecture}
The segmentation backbone is DeepLabV3+~\cite{deeplab}, an encoder-decoder architecture in which the DeepLabV3 encoder uses Atrous Spatial Pyramid Pooling (ASPP) to capture multi-scale features, while the decoder recovers spatial information and refines object boundaries.
The default ResNet-101 encoder is replaced with an EfficientNet-B5 backbone pre-trained on ImageNet.
The EfficientNet family~\cite{efficientnet} jointly scales depth, width and input resolution using a single coefficient $\phi$, while keeping the architecture design fixed; B5 offers a favorable accuracy-to-parameter ratio with respect to the rest of the family.
Additionally, this combination has already been proven effective in~\cite{deeplab+efficientnet}.
\subsection{Additional Modules}
We compare three channel-attention modules placed on top of the backbone: SE, CBAM and GCA. 
EfficientNet-B5 already contains SE blocks inside its building blocks.
\vspace{-0.5em}
\paragraph{Squeeze-and-Excitation (SE)~\cite{se}}
Given an input $\mathbf{X} \in \mathbb{R}^{B \times C \times H \times W}$, SE computes a per-channel descriptor $z \in \mathbb{R}^{C}$ via global average pooling $z_c = \frac{1}{HW}\sum_{i,j} \mathbf{X}(c,i,j)$ and a per-channel weight
\begin{equation}
s = \sigma\big(W_2 \, \mathrm{ReLU}(W_1 z)\big), 
\quad W_1 \in \mathbb{R}^{(C/R) \times C}, \; 
W_2 \in \mathbb{R}^{C \times (C/R)},
\end{equation}
with reduction ratio $R$, producing recalibrated features $\hat{\mathbf{X}} = \mathbf{X} \odot s$.
We add a single SE block at the end of the decoder, just before the classification head.
\vspace{-0.5em}
\paragraph{Convolutional Block Attention Module (CBAM)~\cite{cbam}}
CBAM extends SE to model spatial information.
Channel attention combines average and max pooling through a shared MLP,
\begin{equation}
M_c(\mathbf{X}) = \sigma\big(W_2\,\mathrm{ReLU}(W_1\,\mathrm{AvgPool}(\mathbf{X}))
+ W_2\,\mathrm{ReLU}(W_1\,\mathrm{MaxPool}(\mathbf{X}))\big),
\end{equation}
followed by a spatial map obtained from channel-wise pooling and a $7\times 7$ convolution,
\begin{equation}
M_s(\mathbf{X}') = \sigma\big(f^{7\times 7}\big([\mathrm{AvgPool}(\mathbf{X}'); \mathrm{MaxPool}(\mathbf{X}')]\big)\big).
\end{equation}
The two maps are applied sequentially: $\mathbf{X}' = M_c(\mathbf{X})\odot \mathbf{X}$, $\hat{\mathbf{X}} = M_s(\mathbf{X}')\odot \mathbf{X}'$. 
The spatial component is intended to help localize small nodules.
\vspace{-0.5em}
\paragraph{Grouped Channel Attention (GCA)~\cite{gcaseg}}
GCA partitions $\mathbf{X}$ into $G$ groups along the channel dimension, $\mathbf{X} = [\mathbf{X}_1,\dots,\mathbf{X}_G]$ with $\mathbf{X}_g \in \mathbb{R}^{B\times (C/G)\times H\times W}$ and applies an SE-recalibration independently on each group,
\begin{equation}
s_g = \sigma\big(W_2 \, \mathrm{ReLU}(W_1 z_g)\big), 
\quad W_1 \in \mathbb{R}^{(C/GR) \times (C/G)}, \; 
W_2 \in \mathbb{R}^{(C/G) \times (C/GR)},
\end{equation}
yielding $\hat{\mathbf{X}}_g = \mathbf{X}_g \odot s_g$.
In contrast to~\cite{gcaseg}, where GCA operates on cross-modal features, we evaluate it in a purely visual setting, and we deploy it at three locations in the network: at the encoder bottleneck ($G=16$), on the low-level skip connection feeding the decoder ($G=4$) and at the decoder fusion stage ($G=8$). 
The three locations correspond to high-level context, low-level spatial detail and the fused representation feeding the prediction head.
\subsection{Loss Functions}
The first loss is the standard Binary Cross-Entropy (BCE) loss combined with a Dice term~\cite{bce}.
Given the binary ground-truth mask batch $\mathbf{y}$ and the model logit batch $\mathbf{x}$,
\begin{equation}
\label{BCE}
 \mathcal{L}_{\text{BCE}}(\mathbf{x}, \mathbf{y}) = \mathrm{mean}\{l_1, \dots, l_B\}, \qquad l_i = -\big[\mathbf{y}_i \log\sigma(\mathbf{x}_i) + (1 - \mathbf{y}_i)\log(1 - \sigma(\mathbf{x}_i))\big],
\end{equation}
and the Dice-BCE loss is
\begin{equation}
\label{Dice-BCE}
 \mathcal{L}_{\text{Dice-BCE}} = \mathcal{L}_{\text{BCE}} + \Biggl(1 - \frac{1}{B}\sum_{b=1}^B\frac{\varepsilon + 2 \sum_{h,w} \sigma(x_{b,1,h,w})\, y_{b,1,h,w}}{\varepsilon + \sum_{h,w} \sigma(x_{b,1,h,w}) + y_{b,1,h,w}} \Biggr),
\end{equation}
with smoothing $\varepsilon = 10^{-6}$.
The second loss is the Boundary loss~\cite{boundary}, which penalizes errors more strongly on the nodule frontier, balancing the difference between interior and boundary pixels. 
It is obtained by adding to Eq.~\ref{Dice-BCE} a frontier term $\mathcal{L}_{\text{Frontier}}$ defined as Eq.~\ref{BCE} restricted to boundary pixels (those with a heterogeneous nodule/background neighborhood):
\begin{equation}
 \mathcal{L}_{\text{Boundary}} = \mathcal{L}_{\text{Dice-BCE}} + \lambda \, \mathcal{L}_{\text{Frontier}}, \qquad \lambda \in \mathbb{R}^+.
\end{equation}
\subsection{Training Details}
All models are trained with the same setup: $100$ epochs, batch size $4$, input size $256\times 256$, AdamW optimizer with learning rate $10^{-4}$ and weight decay $10^{-4}$, cosine-annealing schedule with $\eta_{\min}=10^{-6}$ and early stopping with patience $30$ on validation Dice.
The best checkpoint is the one with the highest validation Dice.
All experiments were run on a single NVIDIA T4 GPU (Google Colab Pro) using PyTorch and \texttt{segmentation\_models\_pytorch}.
\subsection{Uncertainty Quantification via Conformal Prediction}
A binary mask does not indicate where the model is confident, a relevant limitation since nodule boundaries are intrinsically blurred and subject to inter-observer disagreement.
We therefore wrap the trained network with a split conformal prediction layer~\cite{cp}, applied post-hoc on the predicted pixel probabilities.
We hold out a subset of the TN3K test set as a calibration set \(\mathcal{C}\), disjoint from the images used for the final evaluation.
For each calibration image, we compute a pixel-level non-conformity score that quantifies the disagreement between the predicted probability and the ground truth.
Given a target error level $\alpha$, the conformal threshold $\hat{\tau}_\alpha$ is set to the $\lceil(1-\alpha)(|\mathcal{C}|+1)\rceil$-th order statistic of the calibration scores and is used at inference to convert sigmoid probabilities into a prediction set per pixel.
Finally, we compute an inner mask of high-confidence nodule pixels and an outer mask that includes pixels with uncertain classification. 
By construction, the procedure provides a marginal coverage guarantee at level $1-\alpha$. 
The visual interpretation is straightforward: the difference between the outer and inner mask is an uncertainty band concentrated where the model is least confident.
