\subsection{Models Comparison}
\begin{figure}[H]
 \centering
 \includegraphics[width=0.85\linewidth]{images/Performances_comparison.png}
 \caption{Qualitative segmentations on two TN3K test cases (one easy, one hard) for the four attention configurations, all trained with Dice-BCE. From left to right: original image, ground truth, prediction.}
 \label{fig:qualitative}
\end{figure}
From Tab.~\ref{tab:segmentation},~\ref{tab:detection} and Fig.~\ref{fig:qualitative} we draw four conclusions.
First, several configurations exceed the $83.77\
by~\cite{prochazka} on the same test set, with our best mDice reaching
$84.84\
DeepLabV3+ combination is already a strong baseline.
Second, no attention mechanism dominates across all metrics. 
Moreover, the per-image median mDice$_{50}$ is approximately $6$ points higher than mDice across all configurations, indicating that the per-image Dice distribution is skewed. The MCC confirms the results given by the Dice coefficients and, as already stated, also the per-image MCC distribution is skewed.
Third, detection rates increase monotonically with nodule size ($\text{DR}_s = 0.891$, $\text{DR}_m = 0.928$, $\text{DR}_l = 0.985$ for CBAM + Dice-BCE).
On the other hand, a performance drop occurs in multi-nodule detection rates, since the DRs are $15$--$24$ percentage points lower on multi-nodule images than on single-nodule ones, consistently across all rows.
The difficulty of multi-nodule segmentation is a known issue in the literature, as qualitatively shown in~\cite{prochazka}; some architectures even explicitly restrict their applicability to the single-nodule setting~\cite{deeplab+efficientnet}.
Fourth, the qualitative comparison in Fig.~\ref{fig:qualitative} shows that in the easy case, all four configurations recover the mask correctly; in the hard, low-contrast case, all of them fail in similar ways.
Considering all metrics jointly, we identify \textbf{CBAM + Dice-BCE} as
the best overall configuration.
\subsection{Uncertainty Visualization}
Fig.~\ref{fig:conformal} shows the conformal prediction output (with $99\
The blue region is the inner prediction set (high-confidence nodule pixels), while the green band is the difference between the outer and inner set, that is, the pixels with uncertain assignment. 
The uncertainty band concentrates along the predicted boundary, while the interior of the nodule is fully classified as certain. 
This is the desired behavior in a clinical scenario: the model is confident within the nodule and uncertain near the boundary.
