\section{Conclusion}
\label{sec:conclusion}
This paper investigated joint active and passive beamforming for an
RIS-assisted backscatter NOMA network operating as the secondary tier of an
underlay cognitive radio system. An interference-normalised decoding order
was adopted to determine the NOMA decoding sequence, allowing the explicit
SIC constraints to be removed from the optimisation problem. The resulting
non-convex weighted-sum-rate maximisation problem was solved using an
alternating optimisation framework combining SDR-based active beamforming
and SROCR-based passive beamforming. Numerical results showed that the
average weighted sum rate converges within approximately $12$ outer AO
iterations, outperforming a fixed-PBF benchmark, and confirmed that
increasing the numbers of BS antennas and RIS elements improves the achievable
weighted sum rate. For the two-user case, a closed-form power allocation was derived
for equal user rates, and its ensemble-average prediction closely
matched the independently observed rate crossover. The results further revealed a fairness--throughput trade-off: operating at
the equal-rate split reduced the aggregate sum rate by about $4.5\,\
supporting the \glspl{bd} reduced the \gls{noma} sum rate by $8.7\,\
to a no-\gls{bd} design.
The reflection-coefficient study showed that increasing $\eta_k$ improves
the backscatter sum rate substantially while the aggregate \gls{noma} rate
remains comparatively insensitive. Taken together, these results show that
the \gls{bd} reflection coefficient and the intra-cluster power allocation
provide complementary controls for balancing backscatter performance,
aggregate throughput, and user fairness. Future work will compare the
proposed design with benchmark schemes, including \gls{oma}, maximum-ratio and
zero-forcing beamforming, and the no-\gls{cr} special case,
and will examine very small and very large $N$, the Rician factor $K_R$, and
robust designs under partial or statistical \gls{csi}.
