\abstract{Multimodal decision models return probabilities over predefined alternatives, offering a structured interface for image classification. This study benchmarks inference with CLEF and CLEF-Flash on 2818 breast ultrasound images from Dataset B, BUSI, and BUS-BRA. Dataset B and BUS-BRA use binary benign-malignant classification, whereas BUSI includes normal, benign, and malignant categories. Model parameters remain unchanged, and original images are submitted without user-defined preprocessing. Three prompts provide minimal labels, an explicit classification task, or sonographic criteria. We report accuracy, macro F1, balanced accuracy, macro precision, throughput, and latency. CLEF-Flash with the clinical prompt achieves the highest accuracy on all three datasets: 74.85\
\keywords{Breast Ultrasound Classification, Decision Models, Multimodal Artificial Intelligence, Inference}
\maketitle
\section{Introduction}
Breast ultrasound is widely used for the examination and characterization of breast lesions because it is noninvasive, accessible, and does not involve ionizing radiation. In parallel with its clinical use, artificial intelligence has been extensively investigated for computer aided breast ultrasound analysis, particularly for distinguishing benign from malignant lesions. Deep learning methods based on convolutional neural networks and, more recently, transformer architectures have achieved promising diagnostic performance \cite{dan2024}. However, conventional approaches generally depend on task specific training data, carefully designed training pipelines, and model optimization for a particular dataset or clinical task. These requirements can limit the portability of a trained model across institutions, acquisition devices, and datasets \cite{minh2026swindeeplabv3}. Recent research therefore increasingly considers more generalizable models that can transfer knowledge across tasks and domains.
Foundation models offer an alternative to developing a separate representation for each imaging task. MedCLIP supports prediction through representations learned from medical images and text \cite{wang2022medclip}, while BiomedCLIP extends biomedical vision-language pretraining to millions of scientific image-text pairs \cite{zhang2024biomedclip}. LLaVA-Med explores visual instruction following for biomedical images \cite{li2023llavamed}. These approaches differ in their training objectives and interfaces, but collectively motivate testing how pretrained multimodal systems respond to medical images and task descriptions at inference time.
A related development is the emergence of promptable vision models. The Segment Anything Model showed that a sufficiently large foundation model could perform segmentation on previously unseen image distributions through prompts rather than conventional task specific retraining \cite{kirillov2023sam}. Its adaptation to medical imaging, MedSAM, was subsequently developed using more than one million medical image mask pairs and demonstrated strong generalization across multiple imaging modalities and anatomical targets \cite{ma2024medsam}. These developments have encouraged a broader transition from designing a separate model for every individual task toward interacting with large pretrained models through prompts. At the same time, specialized foundation models are also being developed for breast ultrasound. For example, BUSGen was pretrained on more than 3.5 million breast ultrasound images and demonstrated strong performance after downstream adaptation \cite{yu2026busgen}. Such approaches illustrate the potential of large scale foundation models, but they also continue to rely on extensive medical domain data and additional task adaptation.
More recently, \textit{decision models} have introduced a structured interface for assigning probabilities to predefined alternatives. Cloudflare's CLEF and CLEF-Flash extend this approach to multimodal inputs \cite{cloudflare2026clef}, motivating an evaluation of their inference capabilities for medical image classification.
Despite rapid progress in multimodal foundation models, the capability of these newly introduced decision models for medical image analysis remains largely unexplored. In particular, their performance when applied directly to breast ultrasound images with task instructions is unclear. Breast ultrasound provides a useful setting for investigating this question because lesion appearance, image quality, acquisition equipment, and operator dependent imaging conditions vary across data sources.
To address this gap, we benchmark inference with CLEF and CLEF-Flash across multiple datasets and prompting strategies. The study focuses on the diagnostic predictions obtained from the released models under a common evaluation protocol.
The main contributions of this study are summarized as follows:
\begin{itemize}
 \item We provide an empirical benchmark of inference with CLEF and CLEF-Flash for breast ultrasound classification.
 \item We investigate three prompting strategies with different levels of task and clinical information to characterize the influence of inference instructions on multimodal decision model performance.
 \item We assess performance across three public breast ultrasound datasets covering binary and three class diagnostic settings.
\end{itemize}
The remainder of this paper is organized as follows. Section~\ref{sec:decision_models} introduces decision models and their architecture. Section~\ref{sec:methods} describes the datasets and inference protocol. Sections~\ref{sec:results} and~\ref{sec:discussion} present and discuss the findings, and Section~\ref{sec:conclusion} concludes the paper.
\section{Decision Models}
\label{sec:decision_models}
Decision models are designed to produce structured outputs over a bounded set of alternatives. TypeSafe describes this interface through its Jev System One model \cite{typesafe2026jev}. The formulation is relevant to classification because it exposes an explicit distribution over the requested labels rather than requiring a label to be extracted from free-form text.
Formally, let $x$ denote an input state and let $Q=\{q_1,q_2,\ldots,q_m\}$ denote a set of decision questions. Each question $q_j$ is associated with a predefined answer space $A_j=\{a_{j1},a_{j2},\ldots,a_{jk}\}$. For a \texttt{choice} question, the official TypeSafe specification defines the output as a probability distribution over these alternatives, with the highest-probability option selected as the answer \cite{typesafe2026choice}. Given the state and decision schema, the model estimates
\begin{equation}
\label{eq:decision_probability}
P(a_{ji}\mid x,q_j), \qquad a_{ji}\in A_j,
\end{equation}
where
\begin{equation}
\sum_{a_{ji}\in A_j} P(a_{ji}\mid x,q_j)=1.
\end{equation}
The predicted decision is then obtained as
\begin{equation}
\hat{a}_j=\arg\max_{a_{ji}\in A_j}P(a_{ji}\mid x,q_j).
\end{equation}
The predicted probability of the selected option is $p_{j,\max}=P(\hat{a}_j\mid x,q_j)$, whereas the meaning of the returned \texttt{confidence} field depends on the implementation. For a \texttt{choice} question with $n_j=|A_j|>1$ alternatives, TypeSafe defines confidence as $(p_{j,\max}-1/n_j)/(1-1/n_j)$: it equals zero for a uniform distribution and one when a single option receives all probability mass \cite{typesafe2026confidence}. Cloudflare's released CLEF implementation instead sets \texttt{confidence} to $p_{j,\max}$ rounded to four decimal places in its \texttt{systemone\_answer} function \cite{cloudflare2026clefconfidence}. Thus, the TypeSafe normalization and the CLEF response field have distinct definitions despite their shared name. TypeSafe further specifies that confidence thresholds should be evaluated on data from the intended use case \cite{typesafe2026confidence}.
This formulation differs from traditional classification heads because the decision alternatives are specified at inference time through a typed schema rather than being permanently fixed during supervised training. As a result, the same pretrained model can be applied to multiple decision tasks without retraining a separate classifier for each label set. The output is also directly interpretable as a probability distribution over bounded alternatives, which makes the framework naturally compatible with medical image classification.
