Figure~\ref{fig:llm_vs_decision_models} illustrates the contrast with autoregressive generation, whose textual outputs may require additional parsing or post processing before use in a classification workflow.
\textbf{Architecture of CLEF and CLEF-Flash}
Figure~\ref{fig:clef_architectures} illustrates the shared architecture of CLEF and CLEF-Flash, including their multimodal backbones and joint schema heads. CLEF and CLEF-Flash share the same backbone-plus-head design, in which a pretrained multimodal language model serves as a frozen encoder and a lightweight decision head replaces the autoregressive language-modeling output \cite{cloudflare2026clef,cloudflare2026clefhf}. CLEF is built on Qwen3.8-27B, whereas CLEF-Flash is built on Qwen3.5-9B. In both models the native vision encoder of the backbone is retained, so images and video frames are converted into visual tokens and processed together with textual tokens in a single sequence. The model input is a serialized record that contains the state $x$ (free text, JSON, images, or video) and the decision schema $Q$. Each question $q_j$ in the schema carries a type (binary \texttt{noul}, nominal \texttt{choice}, or ordinal \texttt{score}), optional natural-language instructions, and a textual description of every admissible option in $A_j$.
Inference proceeds in two stages. In the first stage, the backbone performs a single prefill-only forward pass over the whole record and produces final-layer hidden states $\mathbf{H}\in\mathbb{R}^{T\times d}$, where $T$ is the sequence length and $d$ is the hidden dimension. No token is generated autoregressively. In the second stage, a small transformer module, called the joint schema head, operates on $\mathbf{H}$ through a two-stage attention routing mechanism. First, the representation of each admissible option attends to the state tokens to gather option-specific evidence. Then the question and option representations cross-attend to one another and back to the input payload, so that all questions in the schema are scored jointly rather than independently. A lexical prior derived from the option descriptions helps preserve the semantic meaning of each option. The head outputs one logit $z_{ji}$ for each option $a_{ji}\in A_j$, and the probabilities in Eq.~\ref{eq:decision_probability} are obtained through a per-question softmax:
\begin{equation}
P(a_{ji}\mid x,q_j)=\frac{\exp(z_{ji})}{\sum_{a_{jl}\in A_j}\exp(z_{jl})}.
\end{equation}
The model developers describe post-training with label-smoothed cross-entropy and a Brier loss, followed by reinforcement learning for calibrated decisions \cite{cloudflare2026clef}. This development process is distinct from the present evaluation, which performs inference without updating the released model parameters.
