\section{Materials and Methods}
\label{sec:methods}
\subsection{Experimental Design}
\label{subsec:design}
This study evaluates inference using the publicly released pretrained weights of CLEF and CLEF-Flash. Neither model is trained, fine tuned, or adapted on the evaluated datasets; no task specific classification head is introduced, and all model parameters remain unchanged.
The experimental design crosses three datasets, two decision models, and three prompting strategies, yielding 18 dataset-model-prompt configurations. Figure~\ref{fig:experimental_flow} summarizes the evaluation pipeline.
The original dataset images are used without user-defined resizing, cropping, enhancement, denoising, normalization, segmentation, region-of-interest extraction, or data augmentation. Each image file is Base64-encoded for the inference request payload. This describes processing before submission; it does not imply that the inference engine's native image processor performs no internal transformations.
Ground truth labels are used only after inference for performance evaluation and are never included in the model input.
\subsection{Datasets}
\label{subsec:datasets}
We use Dataset B \cite{yap2018}, BUSI \cite{aldhabyani2020}, and BUS-BRA \cite{gomezflores2024}. Dataset B and BUS-BRA provide binary benign-malignant tasks, while BUSI additionally includes normal images. The evaluated sets contain 163, 780, and 1875 images, respectively, for a total of 2818 images. Table~\ref{tab:datasets} reports the class counts used in this evaluation. Metrics are computed separately for each dataset.
