\subsection{Evaluation Metrics}
\label{subsec:metrics}
We assess classification performance using accuracy, F1 score, balanced accuracy, and precision, with the breast ultrasound evaluation study of Tan et al.~\cite{TAN2026114134} as a related reference. Accuracy is the proportion of correctly classified images. For each class $c$, precision is $P_c=TP_c/(TP_c+FP_c)$, recall is $R_c=TP_c/(TP_c+FN_c)$, and F1 score is their harmonic mean, $F1_c=2P_cR_c/(P_c+R_c)$, where $TP_c$, $FP_c$, and $FN_c$ denote true positives, false positives, and false negatives under a one-versus-rest interpretation. Balanced accuracy is the mean recall across the $K$ classes, $K^{-1}\sum_{c=1}^{K}R_c$. Precision and F1 score are reported as unweighted means of their class-specific values (macro averages), and all four classification metrics are expressed as percentages.
Inference efficiency is summarized by frames per second (FPS), the number of images processed per second, and latency, the mean elapsed time per image in milliseconds. For $N$ images processed sequentially over a measured inference interval of $T$ seconds, $\mathrm{FPS}=N/T$ and mean latency is $1000T/N$ ms/image. Higher classification scores and FPS indicate better performance, whereas lower latency indicates faster inference.
