\subsection{Class-Specific Error Patterns}
Figures~\ref{fig:cm_busb}-\ref{fig:cm_busbra} in Appendix~\ref{app:confusion_matrices} show the confusion matrices. On Dataset B, changing CLEF from P1 to P3 reduces malignant cases predicted as benign from 16 to 5, but increases benign cases predicted as malignant from 32 to 52. For CLEF-Flash, the corresponding counts change from 15 to 10 and from 28 to 31. On BUSI, CLEF-Flash with P3 correctly identifies 65 normal and 260 benign images, compared with 51 and 235 under P1, while correctly identified malignant images decrease from 202 to 192. CLEF with P2 identifies the most normal images (83 of 133), helping explain its stronger balanced accuracy.
On BUS-BRA, errors are dominated by benign images predicted as malignant. CLEF with P3 correctly classifies 592 of 607 malignant images but only 164 of 1268 benign images. CLEF-Flash with P3 correctly classifies 550 malignant and 418 benign images. The resulting malignant recall is 97.53\
\section{Discussion}
\label{sec:discussion}
\subsection{Model and Prompt Dependence}
The results show that a larger model does not consistently provide better classification in this evaluation. CLEF-Flash outperforms CLEF under every matched prompt on the two binary datasets, while CLEF retains an advantage in macro F1 and balanced accuracy on BUSI with P2. Model selection therefore depends on both the dataset and the evaluation criterion. These comparisons concern two released models with different backbones and do not isolate parameter count as the cause of their performance differences.
The effect of clinical instructions is also model dependent. Moving from P1 to P3 improves CLEF-Flash accuracy on all three datasets, although Dataset B shows a small decrease at P2. For CLEF, the same change reduces accuracy on every dataset. The confusion matrices show that P3 shifts CLEF toward malignant predictions, increasing malignant recall at the expense of benign recall. These observations support evaluating prompts empirically rather than assuming that additional clinical detail will improve every aspect of classification. They do not establish whether the models use the described sonographic features as intended.
\subsection{Dataset Variation and Class Imbalance}
BUS-BRA exposes a substantial limitation of direct inference. Given its 1268 benign images among 1875 images, predicting every image as benign would yield 67.63\
Performance differences across datasets suggest sensitivity to the evaluated data source, but these experiments do not isolate its cause. Acquisition conditions, image composition, and label characteristics are possible contributors that require separate analysis. BUSI also uses three classes rather than two, so differences in its aggregate scores cannot be attributed solely to dataset shift.
\subsection{Inference Efficiency}
CLEF-Flash offers lower observed latency across all matched comparisons, with approximately 3.30-3.64-fold speedups on Dataset B, 1.85-1.91-fold on BUSI, and 1.30-1.38-fold on BUS-BRA. These measurements support its efficiency advantage in the reported runs, but latency remains above two seconds per image even for the fastest configuration. The reported measurements apply to the A100/Ollama configuration described in Section~\ref{subsec:models}. These latency estimates are specific to the reported environment.
\subsection{Scope and Limitations}
The present comparisons are descriptive: the table provides point estimates without confidence intervals or paired significance tests. Small differences, such as the 0.51 percentage-point accuracy gap between CLEF-Flash/P3 and CLEF/P1 on BUSI, should not be interpreted as established superiority. The aggregate evaluation also does not establish probability calibration or the reliability of confidence-based selection. These require predictions linked to reference labels and an explicitly verified confidence definition. The observed results concern image-level classification; they do not establish patient-level diagnostic performance or clinical utility. The evaluation does not determine whether related images appeared in the models' original training data, so unchanged parameters during this study should not be equated with demonstrated absence of prior data exposure.
No supervised ultrasound model, biomedical foundation model, or radiologist was evaluated under the same protocol. Consequently, the results characterize the two decision models rather than establish superiority over existing diagnostic approaches. The image-level confusion matrices also do not account for within-patient dependence where multiple images are available. Future evaluation should include matched baselines, patient-aware uncertainty estimates, and calibration analysis.
\section{Conclusion}
\label{sec:conclusion}
This study evaluated inference with CLEF and CLEF-Flash across three breast ultrasound datasets and three prompting strategies. CLEF-Flash with P3 achieved the highest accuracy on each dataset and led all reported metrics on Dataset B and BUS-BRA, while CLEF with P2 achieved the strongest macro F1 and balanced accuracy on BUSI. CLEF-Flash was consistently faster in the reported environment. Nevertheless, poor benign recall on BUS-BRA and the contrasting response of the two models to clinical instructions show that structured outputs alone do not ensure reliable diagnostic classification. The benchmark supports evaluating dataset-specific errors, prompt sensitivity, and inference cost together. Matched baselines and patient-level validation are needed before broader conclusions about clinical use can be drawn.
