\section{Conclusion}
\label{sec:CONCLUSIONS}
This work proposed a radiomics-based pipeline for 
thyroid nodule malignancy classification, integrating a GAM classifier, 
selected among six candidates for its best trade-off between 
discriminative performance and interpretability, with label-conditional 
cross-conformal prediction for statistically guaranteed uncertainty 
quantification. The GAM achieved competitive classification 
performance and conformal prediction provided 
clinically relevant uncertainty estimates, supporting high-confidence 
malignancy predictions while referring ambiguous cases to the clinician.
Class imbalance emerged as the main limiting factor, affecting classifier specificity and NPV, and preventing label-conditional 
coverage at $\epsilon=0.10$ for the underrepresented benign class. 
At $\epsilon=0.09$, the model achieved high conditional precision 
but lower conditional NPV, and 37\
clinician, suggesting that the framework only partially addresses the problem of reducing unnecessary biopsies. The reliance 
on a single dataset further limited the generalizability of these results 
to different acquisition settings and patient populations. Additionally, 
radiomic features were extracted from bounding box crops rather than precise nodule segmentations, which may introduce noise from surrounding 
thyroid tissue and limit feature accuracy.
As a work in progress, future developments will focus on addressing class imbalance both at the classifier training stage and through conformal prediction methods more robust to skewed classes. Further directions will include multi-dataset validation, integration of nodule segmentation prior to feature extraction, and deeper clinical interpretation of radiomic features.
