\section{Introduction}
In 2024, TB was once again the world's leading cause of death from a single infectious agent~\cite{tb_report}.
Accessible, scalable and cost-effective screening and triage tools for TB therefore remain a priority, especially in Africa, a region accounting for 25\
The application of basic clinical data or results from laboratory-based tests as a method to identify people with TB has been reported in the literature, including the application of various machine learning approaches~\cite{Bartl2025-jo,Orjuela-Canon2025-eh,botha2018,Ghermi2024-zv,Parihar2025}.
Recently, handheld point-of-care ultrasound (POCUS) devices have become readily available in many clinical settings~\cite{Lee2020}.
Their use in echocardiography and emergency medicine, for example, is well established.
POCUS has been employed for use in identifying haemothorax~\cite{Brooks44} and pneumothorax~\cite{Zhang2006} in people with thoracic trauma.
Ultrasonography of the lung relies on the identification of specific patterns to enable similar insights, for example the identification of comet-tails, lung sliding, and anechoic areas~\cite{Staub2018,Lichtenstein1995,Lichtenstein1998}.
It is possible, therefore, that deep-learning image-based analysis  may be able to accomplish these tasks automatically, as they are strong pattern recognition systems.
Indeed, some deep-learning based approaches developed during the COVID-19 pandemic have achieved high performance~\cite{jimaging8030065,KHAN2023106994}.
For example, in a cohort of  $n=\ $48 patients admitted to ICU with COVID-19 like symptoms, and for whom 692 lung ultrasound (LUS) scans were captured, an AI-based tool (ExoLungAI) was able to identify A-lines with 0.91 sensitivity and 0.81 specificity and B-lines with 0.84 sensitivity and 0.86 specificity~\cite{diagnostics14222526}.
Similarly, when applied to $n=\ $28 COVID-19 positive patients, a deep-learning based model was shown to have high agreement with a human expert in identifying lung pathologies such as B-lines, consolidations and pleural effusion.
This model was also shown to effectively augment the examination process by reducing the average time required from 12.6 to 5.3 minutes~\cite{ijtm2010002}.
Finally, a sensitivity of 0.88 and a specificity of 0.94  was achieved when employing VGG to classify frames from LUS scans of 1204 COVID-19 patients against similar data from 2030 people who were either healthy or had bacterial pneumonia~\cite{app11020672}. 
However, when this same model is applied to a group of $n=\ $66 patients, 33 of whom are positive for COVID-19, a sensitivity of 0.62 and specificity of 0.7 was achieved, showcasing the challenge of developing such systems which are robust to applications in unseen settings.
After fine-tuning, a sensitivity of 0.9 and specificity of 0.55 ~\cite{covid_blues} was achieved.
LUS shows promise as a practical screening modality, as the handheld devices are both mobile and more cost-effective than classical ultrasound devices~\cite{BARIBEAU20203139}.
However, their effectiveness for the detection of pulmonary TB is to date unclear, and further research is needed~\cite{ptb_diagnostic_accuracy_review}.
To our knowledge, only two studies have considered the application of deep-learning to predict TB from LUS.
The first employed a CNN-LSTM with a cohort of $n=\ $710 patients to classify bacteriologically or clinically confirmed TB from 11455 videos, achieving a sensitivity of 0.92 and specificity of 0.73 (AUROC=0.89) in a test set of 156 people~\cite{swinkels2025prototype}.
The second, a prospective study employing the same data used in this work, found a ResNet-based TB classifier trained on still images captured during a LUS exam to afford an AUROC of 0.91 [0.89,0.93] (95\
In this work we make the following contributions.
\begin{itemize}
 \item We show that ResNet-based models afford similar performance when trained on a composite reference standard of bacteriologically and clinically diagnosed TB to previous work using only bacteriologically confirmed TB.
 \item We demonstrate that classifiers based only on clinical and demographic data yield reasonable performance.
 \item We demonstrate that small but consistent improvements are seen when clinical and LUS classifiers are fused.
 \item We show that the simple averaging of the output probabilities from the two classifiers outperforms more complex ways to incorporate the clinical feature vector directly into the LUS classifier.
\end{itemize}
\section{Data}
The dataset we employ comprises $n=\ $502 patients presenting with respiratory symptoms to the National Teaching Hospital for Tuberculosis and Pulmonary Diseases (CNHU-PPC) in Benin~\cite{ultrai2025escmid,suttels2022imagebanks}.
POCUS exams were conducted using a standardised 14-point sliding protocol and images were captured.
TB status was determined using a sputum-based Xpert MTB/RIF Ultra~\cite{Dorman2017} test.
Additionally, participants classified with clinically diagnosed TB (based on clinical, radiological, and outcome data) are treated as positive in this cohort.
This inclusion of clinically diagnosed TB differentiates the work presented here from previous results on the same dataset, in which only Ultra results were used to determine TB.
Data is partitioned into five folds for cross-validation, with an explicit fixed test set.
