\section{Experimental setup}
All experiments are conducted within a five-fold cross-validation scheme, where training occurs using four of the five folds and the fifth is held-out for development.
Hyperparameters are chosen based on the average development set loss across the five folds, fixed, and the development set re-introduced for final training on all five folds.
This final classifier is then applied to the unseen test set.
For all models, the AUROC is reported for the development and test sets.
A bootstrapped confidence interval of the AUROC is constructed for development sets by resampling 2000 times over the five sets, each time recomputing the average development set AUROC utilising the Mann-Whitney formulation.
From this empirical distribution, the values for the 2.5th and 97.5th percentile are determined~\cite{efron1994introduction}.
The reported confidence interval on the test set uses the DeLong-based method~\cite{delong_1988}.
\subsection{Clinical data classifier}
We evaluate both a simple logistic regression (LR) model as well as a 2 or 3 layer multi-layer perceptron (MLP) which receive as input the encoded clinical data as a feature vector.
Quantitative features are used directly, while binary or one-hot encoding is used for categorical variables, as denoted in Table~\ref{tab:clinical_features}.
Mean and variance normalisation, based on the training set, is applied to all feature vectors.
Both models are optimised over the hyperparameters listed in Table~\ref{tab:hyperparameters}.
After subsequent feature selection, hyperparameters are re-optimised over the same ranges.
The hyperparameters for the clinical-based models are not jointly optimised during feature selection due to the associated computational complexity.
