 Fig. \ref{comp_time} shows the root sum MSE and the computational time of the proposed framework and the benchmark schemes versus the number of targets. It is observed that our proposed framework outperforms all schemes and achieves the same performance as that of marginal MAP and MMSE with numerical calculation of posterior PDF, while it consumes significantly less computation time compared to its numerical calculation based counterparts due to the closed-form expression of the posterior PDF, especially as $M$ becomes large. Fig. \ref{MSE} evaluates the root sum MSE of various schemes versus the receive SNR under $M=2$. It is observed that our proposed framework outperforms all schemes except for MMSE with numerically calculated posterior PDF, and approaches the latter as well as the PCRB in the moderate-to-high receive SNR regime. These results validate the effectiveness of our proposed framework. 

 Next, we evaluate the performance of progressive sensing under our proposed framework. Fig. \ref{multistage_all} shows the update of prior PDFs and the angle estimates. It is observed that the surrogate posterior PDFs approximate the posterior PDFs well and gradually concentrate around the actual targets' angles as the sensing stage progresses, leading to more accurate estimates. The Bayesian beamforming also tends to concentrate more power around highly-probable angles progressively. Moreover, even when the ESPRIT initialization provides inaccurate estimates, our proposed framework effectively improves them by exploiting the surrogate posterior PDFs. Finally, Fig. \ref{RMSE_stage} shows the sensing performance with different observation windows. It is observed that enlarging the observation window with smaller $\rho(s)$ improves the sensing accuracy at a cost of higher complexity.
\vspace{-5mm}\section{Conclusions}
\vspace{-3mm}
This paper proposed a variational Bayesian inference based progressive multi-target MIMO sensing framework with nuisance parameters. In each sensing stage, the BS sends beamformed probing signals designed to minimize the sum conditional PCRB for all angles based on the prior PDF, which are reflected by targets and received back at the BS as observations. Then, a variational Bayesian inference based approach was proposed to derive a closed-form surrogate posterior PDF, which is used for Bayesian sensing and Bayesian beamforming in the subsequent stage. The proposed framework only has polynomial complexity over the number of targets, which makes it highly scalable. Numerical results verified the performance and complexity advantages of our proposed framework and demonstrated the progressive refinement of sensing performance.
