 &\hspace{2cm}
 \leq
 C_M
 (1-2\rho)^{-\frac{M-1}{2}}
 \epsilon^{\frac{M-1}{2}},
\end{align}
where $C_M = \frac{M^{\frac{M}{2}}}{2^{M-1}\pi^{\frac{M-1}{2}}\Gamma\left(\frac{M+1}{2}\right)}$, and $\Gamma(\cdot)$ is the Gamma function.
Since $\rho>0$ is arbitrary, letting $\rho\to0$ gives $\Pr\{\mathcal D_\epsilon\}
 \sim
 C_M
 \epsilon^{\frac{M-1}{2}}.$ Using the small-angle expansion $2\sin^2\left(\frac{x}{2}\right)
 =
 \frac{x^2}{2}
 +
 \mathcal O(x^4)$, and the fact that $x=\mathcal O(\sqrt{\epsilon})$ in the high-gain
