\begin{abstract}
Inflammatory bowel disease (IBD) requires frequent imaging-based assessment, yet interpretation of modalities such as wireless capsule endoscopy (WCE) and intestinal ultrasound remains heavily dependent on specialist expertise. Vision-Language Models (VLMs) demonstrate significant potential in multimodal medical image analysis, but their clinical adoption is hindered by their insufficient domain-specific reasoning, susceptibility to hallucination, scarcity of good quality training data in fine-grained diagnostics and limited interpretability. We introduce ARISE (Adaptive Agentic Reasoning with Image-grounded Self-Evaluation), an autonomous planning framework that models few-shot medical image understanding as a sequential agentic workflow. ARISE structures agent execution into a transparent 5-stage workflow: hypothesis generation, image-grounded evidence summarization, evidence-conditioned refinement, symbolic verification, and final diagnosis. We apply ARISE to IBD assessment across two independent patient cohorts: wireless capsule endoscopy (WCE) images for Crohn's disease and B-Mode ultrasound data for Ulcerative Colitis. ARISE consistently improves diagnostic performance over baseline VLMs while exposing where reasoning succeeds or fails, providing a more interpretable basis for clinical decision support and realistic deployment.
\end{abstract}
\section{Introduction}
Inflammatory bowel disease (IBD), including Crohn’s disease (CD) and ulcerative colitis (UC), is a chronic relapsing condition that requires repeated assessment of disease activity to prevent progressive bowel damage and other complications \cite{ng2017worldwide, turner2021stride2, torres2017crohns}. Two imaging modalities are central to this monitoring workflow. Wireless capsule endoscopy (WCE) \cite{iddan2000wireless} provides direct visualization of small-bowel mucosal abnormalities in Crohn’s disease, while intestinal ultrasound (IUS) \cite{maaser2020trustuc} offers a radiation-free means of assessing bowel-wall features such as colon wall thickening and loss of stratification, both established markers of activity in ulcerative colitis \cite{bots2018ultrasound}. Despite their clinical value, both modalities share a critical bottleneck: accurate interpretation depends heavily on scarce, subspecialty-trained experts \cite{vallee2020crohnipi, leenings2025ctrus}. In resource-limited and non-tertiary settings, this dependence delays diagnosis and monitoring precisely where timely intervention matters most \cite{kaplan2021four}.
Vision-language models (VLMs) offer a promising way to support medical image interpretation without task-specific training \cite{radford2021clip, alayrac2022flamingo, liu2023llava, openai2023gpt4}. Yet their translation into IBD assessment is hindered by four well-documented obstacles \cite{hager2024_llm_clinical_decision}. First, general-purpose VLMs exhibit a domain-reasoning gap: fine-grained IBD diagnosis requires integrating subtle, co-occurring visual cues over disease progression rather than recognizing salient objects, and these cues are underrepresented in general training distributions \cite{du2025_mki_prompt_tuning,zhang2024disease}. Second, VLMs remain prone to hallucination and to internally contradictory rationales, an unacceptable failure mode in a clinical setting \cite{ji2023hallucination, li2025treble, madaan2023selfrefine}. Third, high-quality annotated IBD imaging data are scarce, ruling out conventional fine-tuning in most practical deployments \cite{shakeri2024fewshot, vallee2020crohnipi}. 
Finally, even accurate VLMs act as black boxes, providing no way to distinguish sound visual reasoning from chance or spurious shortcut \cite{Byun2025TestTimeScaling, byun2026medicalvqacalibration}; inference-time methods such as test-time prompt tuning and retrieval-augmented in-context learning avoid retraining, but do not necessarily produce reasoning that can be inspected or verified\cite{shu2022tpt, ram2023incontextralm, zhan2026retrieval, xia2025mmedrag}. 
Neurosymbolic methods that translate natural-language problems into symbolic form for solver-backed verification improve logical faithfulness, but they have been developed for text-only reasoning rather than image-grounded medical diagnosis, where hypotheses must be tied to observable visual findings \cite{pan2023logiclm, olausson2023linc, garcez2023neurosymbolicai}. Moreover, most medical-agent evaluations emphasize final-task accuracy, making it difficult to identify where failures arise within a multi-stage reasoning process \cite{liu2023mmbench, fu2025mmebenchmark, hager2024_llm_clinical_decision}. As recent workflow-aware benchmarks have argued, final-output metrics alone cannot reveal where or why an agent fails \cite{schmidgall2024agentclinic, jiang2025medagentbench}. In short, no existing few-shot adaptation approach produces a reasoning state that is simultaneously grounded in the image evidence, verified for internal consistency, and open to expert inspection before deployment.
We introduce ARISE (Adaptive Agentic Reasoning with Image-grounded Self-Evaluation), an autonomous framework that adapts a frozen VLM to few-shot IBD assessment by constructing, grounding, verifying, and refining an explicit set of diagnostic hypotheses, and that evaluates both its intermediate reasoning stages and its final diagnosis. ARISE follows five stages: (S1) generation of diagnostic hypotheses from task and clinical knowledge, (S2) image-grounded summarization of findings from a small support set, (S3) evidence-conditioned refinement of those hypotheses, (S4) symbolic verification and repair using first-order logic and an SMT solver, and (S5) final diagnosis of unseen images. This design makes transparency and correctness mutually reinforcing, and constrains hallucination without fine-tuning. Each stage produces an inspectable artifact and an associated score, allowing ARISE to evaluate both the quality of its own reasoning process and the accuracy of the resulting diagnosis evaluated in two settings: Wireless Capsule Endoscopy (WCE) for Crohn’s disease and B-mode ultrasound for ulcerative colitis.
Our contributions are fourfold: First, we introduce a few-shot adaptation strategy in which domain knowledge is represented as image-grounded, iteratively refined hypotheses rather than learned parameters. Second, we extend solver-backed neurosymbolic reasoning to image-grounded medical diagnosis by verifying and repairing VLM-generated hypotheses before prediction. Third, we introduce stage-level evaluation that separates agentic process quality from task performance, revealing where reasoning failures occur rather than reducing evaluation to a final label. Finally, we demonstrate the framework across independent WCE \cite{vallee2020crohnipi} and B-mode ultrasound \cite{leenings2025ctrus} cohorts and multiple VLM families, showing that structured, verifiable reasoning can improve diagnostic performance across modalities and model backbones.
\section{Methodology}
\label{sec:method}
\begin{figure*}
 \centering
 \includegraphics[width=0.8\linewidth]{figures/pipeline.png}
 \caption{An overview of the ARISE workflow for the Crohn's disease WCE Pathological vs Non-Pathological image classification. The workflow starts with initial hypotheses generation and a parallel summarization of visual cues from select examples. The initial hypotheses are first updated using the summary of visual cues, followed by a logical consistency check via an SMT solver. The final hypotheses are used for the final diagnosis stage. }
 \label{fig:pipeline}
\end{figure*}

We describe \textbf{ARISE} (\emph{Adaptive Agentic Reasoning with Image-grounded Self-Evaluation}), an autonomous agent that adapts a frozen vision-language model (VLM) to few-shot inflammatory bowel disease (IBD) assessment by executing a transparent, five-stage reasoning workflow. Unlike a single opaque prompt, ARISE carries an explicit workflow \emph{state} across stages, invokes an external symbolic tool, and emits an
inspectable artifact at every stage that receives its own \emph{score}. No VLM parameters are updated: adaptation is stored entirely as an auditable hypothesis state. This eliminates the dependency on massive datasets, bypassing the conventional fine-tuning bottleneck that typically hinders medical AI deployment. We summarize a run as an \emph{agentic} (process) score over the five workflow stages plus a \emph{task} (outcome) score, and combine them into a single overall score.

\subsection{Problem Formulation}
\label{sec:problem}

Let $\mathcal{D}=\{(x_i,y_i)\}_{i=1}^{N}$ be an IBD imaging
dataset, where $x_i$ is an input image (a wireless capsule endoscopy (WCE) frame for the Crohn's cohort or a B-mode ultrasound frame for the ulcerative colitis (UC) cohort) and $y_i\in\mathcal{Y}$ is a diagnostic label. We operate in the few-shot regime: a small labeled support set $\mathcal{S}=\{(x_j,y_j)\}_{j=1}^{m}$ is available for adaptation, and a disjoint
query set $\mathcal{Q}$ is held out. Each task carries a task description $T$
(modality, label space $\mathcal{Y}$, acquisition context, requested output) and a clinical cue description $C$ enumerating expert knowledge about the visual findings associated with each label.

The \textbf{ARISE agent} is the tuple $(M_\theta,\pi,V,\Gamma)$: $M_\theta$ is
the frozen VLM; $\pi$ is a deterministic controller that sequences the stages and triggers repair loops; $V$ is a symbolic satisfiability-modulo-theories (SMT) verifier (Z3); and $\Gamma$ is a prompt-based parser that maps each
natural-language hypothesis to a typed first-order-logic (FOL) premise. The agent maintains a state
\begin{equation}
\sigma^{(t)}=\bigl(H^{(t)},\ \Phi^{(t)},\ \mathcal{E}^{(t)},\ \mathbf{z}^{(t)}\bigr),
\end{equation}
comprising the hypothesis set $H^{(t)}$, its premises
$\Phi^{(t)}=\Gamma(H^{(t)})$, an evidence memory $\mathcal{E}^{(t)}$, and the
vector of stage scores $\mathbf{z}^{(t)}=(s_1,\dots,s_5)$. The exact scores are described in details in Appendix A. The goal is to construct a verified hypothesis state $H^{\ast}$ that yields an accurate query prediction $\hat{y}=f_{\mathrm{task}}(x,H^{\ast},\mathcal{E}^{\ast},M_\theta)$
while producing an inspectable, individually-scored reasoning trace.

\subsection{Stage S1: Hypothesis Generation}
\label{sec:w1}

ARISE composes a planning prompt from task and clinical knowledge,
$p_0=\mathrm{Plan}(T,C,\mathcal{Y})$, and the VLM generates concise, checkable
hypotheses  
\begin{equation}
H^{(0)}=\{h_1,\dots,h_K\}=M_\theta(p_0;\ \tau,p_{\mathrm{top}}),
\end{equation}
where the sampling temperature $\tau$ and nucleus sampling parameter $p_{\text{top}}$ can be varied to encourage hypothesis diversity. For example, in the WCE six-lesion task, $p_0$ may ask: ``Given Crohn's disease capsule endoscopy images and the label set $\{\mathrm{AU}, \mathrm{E}, \mathrm{O}, \mathrm{S}, \mathrm{U3-10}, \mathrm{U{>}10}\}$, generate concise visual hypotheses linking observable mucosal findings to each label. Consider mucosal breaks, white ulcer base, red halo, diffuse redness, enlarged villi, and luminal narrowing. Avoid claims that cannot be checked from the image.''

\subsection{Stage S2: Image-Grounded Evidence Summarization}
\label{sec:w2}

ARISE initializes an empty evidence memory $\mathcal{E}^{(0)}=\varnothing$ and picks one example image at a time, sequentially. At step $t$ it selects $(x_t,y_t)$ and
asks the VLM to report only the \emph{observable} evidence in $x_t$ supporting
$y_t$ under the current hypotheses:
\begin{equation}
e_t=M_\theta\bigl(\mathrm{Observe}(x_t,y_t,p_0)\bigr).
\end{equation}
The output is \emph{structured}: $e_t$ tags each cited finding against a
per-cohort controlled vocabulary $\mathcal{V}$ (for endoscopy, this will be mucosal break, white ulcer base, red halo, diffuse erythema, enlarged villi, luminal narrowing, etc.). This makes scoring a set comparison rather than text parsing, which is simpler to evaluate. The generated visual summary is appended as
$\mathcal{E}^{(t)}=\mathcal{E}^{(t-1)}\cup\{e_t\}$. The final evidence set is denoted as $\mathcal{E}^*$.
The grounding score \(s_2\) measures agreement between model-identified findings and expert-annotated findings, emphasizing visual fidelity over unsupported inference.
\subsection{Stage S3: Evidence-Conditioned Refinement}
\label{sec:w4}

Given the accumulated evidence, the agent proposes a refined set
$H^{(\mathcal{E}^*)}=M_\theta(\mathrm{Refine}(p_0,H^{(0)},\mathcal{E}^*))$. The refinement score \(s_3\) measures whether the refined hypothesis set improves downstream prediction performance relative to the initial hypotheses on a held-out validation subset. 
