 \centering
 \includegraphics[width=1\linewidth]{figures/Summaryrefinement.png}
 \caption{Example of stages S2 and S3 on the Crohn's disease Ulceration Diagnosis task with GPT-5.4}
 \label{fig:refinementexample}
\end{figure}
Stages S2 and S3 are jointly described in Figure \ref{fig:refinementexample}. 
\subsection{Stage S4: Symbolic Verification}
\label{sec:w3}

The generated hypotheses $H^{(\mathcal{E}^*)}$ are converted from natural language to first order logic statements $\Phi$ using an LLM parser $\Gamma$,  $\Phi = \Gamma(H^{(\mathcal{E}^*)})$. For example, the natural language statement "A mucosal break with a white base indicates aphthoid ulceration" is translated to the form $(\text{MucosalBreak}(x) \wedge \text{WhiteBase}(x)) \rightarrow \text{AU}$. These are then passed into a Satisfiability Modulo Theories Solver.
\begin{equation}
V(\Phi)=
\begin{cases}
1,& \text{if } \Phi \text{ is satisfiable},
0,& \text{otherwise.}
\end{cases}
\end{equation}
If $V(\Phi)=0$, $V$ returns the minimal conflicting set $b$ and the LLM repairs the hypotheses,
$H^{*}=M_\theta(\mathrm{Repair}(p_0,H^{(\mathcal{E}^*)},b))$, giving the final hypotheses set $H^{*}$ to be used for diagnosis.  
The verification score \(s_4\) measures the fidelity of the natural-language-to-logic translation, evaluated through bidirectional entailment between the original and reconstructed hypotheses. This is done using a natural language inference model, details are provided in the Appendix A. 

\subsection{Stage S5: Final Diagnosis}
\label{sec:w5}

With the verified state $H^{\ast}$, the model performs a downstream classification task, classifying an input $\hat{x}$ with a corresponding label $\hat{y}$. A few examples of pictures and labels are added for in-context learning. For each classification, we also output the hypothesis ${h}$ it uses for the classification. The stage score \(s_5\) measures whether the prediction belongs to the valid label space and whether the model grounds its prediction in the verified hypothesis set.
The task score for the entire agentic system is measured as the average of the classification accuracy, F1-score, precision, and recall on $\mathcal{Q}$.

\subsection{Workflow-Aware Scoring: Agentic $+$ Task}
\label{sec:scoring}

The five workflow stages are aggregated into a single \emph{agentic} (process) score, and the outcome is the \emph{task} score:
\begin{align}
\textsc{Agentic} &= \sum_{k=1}^{5} w_k\,s_k,\qquad \sum_{k=1}^{5} w_k=1, 
\textsc{Overall} &= \tfrac{1}{2}\,\textsc{Agentic}+\tfrac{1}{2}\,\textsc{Task}.
\end{align}
Weights emphasize the trust-critical stages, with grounding ($s_2$) assigned the highest weight and symbolic verification and refinement the second highest; our default is $\mathbf{w}=(w_1,\dots,w_5)=(0.20,0.30,0.20,0.20,0.10)$.



\section{Experiments}
\subsection{Datasets and Tasks}

ARISE is evaluated across two IBD imaging domains: Crohn’s disease (CD) using wireless capsule endoscopy (WCE) and ulcerative colitis (UC) using B-mode intestinal ultrasound. Detailed dataset composition, support/few-shot examples/test splits, and task definitions are provided in Appendix B.

For Crohn's disease, we use the CrohnIPI dataset \cite{vallee2020crohnipi}, a wireless capsule endoscopy (WCE) collection of 3,498 RGB images independently reviewed by multiple clinical experts. We evaluate three tasks of increasing diagnostic granularity: (1) Pathological vs non-pathological image classification, (2) ulceration diagnosis (ulceration vs. no ulceration), and (3) six-lesion classification for pathological images, with labels being aphthoid ulceration, stenosis, edema, erythema, ulceration between $3$ and $10$ mm, and ulceration \(>10\) mm.
For Ulcerative Colitis, ultrasound data is taken from the C-TRUS dataset \cite{leenings2025ctrus}, containing a total of 800 ultrasound images collected from 13 patients. We evaluate colon-wall stratification status, classifying whether bowel-wall layering is preserved or lost, a clinically relevant marker of intestinal inflammation.

\subsection{Models and Prompting}

We evaluate ARISE using instruction-following VLMs from three model families: GPT, Gemini, and Qwen. We test GPT-5.4 \cite{afridi2026gpt}, GPT-4o-mini \cite{openai2023gpt4}, Gemini-2.5 \cite{comanici2025gemini25pushingfrontier}, and Qwen2-VL \cite{wang2024qwen2vlenhancingvisionlanguagemodels}, with Qwen2-VL serving as an open-weight VLM baseline. 
All models are evaluated on identical query splits. Automatic hypothesis generation and logical premise construction use a reasoning-oriented language model, while symbolic contradiction detection is performed using the Z3 SMT solver~\cite{demoura2008z3}.
We perform 2 major experiments. 1. We compare the diagnostic accuracy, F1 score, precision, and recall of the base VLM and the VLM with the final generated hypotheses (task score after stage 5). 2. We then compare the agentic scores for every stage across models, and the overall task score for every model, on the 6-lesion classification task. This would tell us which stages are strongest and weakest, and which models tend to outperform the others in both workflow and task-level performance. Crucially, these evaluations jointly establish the clinical value of verifiable reasoning over standard prompting, while opening the black box to reveal exactly how process-level reasoning quality drives final diagnostic accuracy.
\section{Results}
\subsection{Diagnostic Performance: Base VLM vs. ARISE}
Table \ref{tab:combined_accuracy} compares base-model accuracy with the same VLMs operating within the ARISE workflow across the three Crohn’s disease WCE tasks. ARISE improves accuracy for every model, with the largest accuracy gain observed as the task complexity increases (6-lesion classification being the hardest) and for the weaker baseline models. Qwen2-VL and Gemini-2.5 close much of the gap to the stronger GPT baselines once wrapped in the ARISE workflow, suggesting that the framework's gains come from structuring and verifying reasoning rather than from any one backbone's raw visual capability. Detailed task-level results for this dataset, including accuracy, F1 score, precision, and recall for all model–task combinations, are provided in the Appendix.
