\subsection{Stage-Level Reasoning Quality}
Figure \ref{fig:stagewiseagenticscores} reports the five individual stage scores (s1 through s5) for all four models on the six-lesion classification task, together with the mean across models at each stage.
Hypothesis generation (S1) and symbolic verification (S4) are consistently strong across models, while evidence-guided refinement (S3) is the most challenging stage and exhibits the greatest model variation. The contrast between the two kinds of stages points to a broader pattern: agents are consistently better at rule-based steps than at visually-grounded refinement, where they must revisit the image evidence itself and use it to revise their own hypotheses rather than follow a fixed procedure. The strongest models also perform better during refinement and final diagnosis, indicating that visually grounded adaptation is a key factor separating stronger from weaker agents.
