\subsection{Agentic and Task-level scores across models}
We examine how each model's intermediate reasoning quality (the agentic score) relates to its final classification performance (the task score) on the six-lesion classification task. Figure \ref{fig:overallagenttaskscores} reports the overall, agentic, and task scores for all four models, ranked by overall score.
Across models, stronger agentic performance generally corresponds to stronger diagnostic performance, but the relationship is not one-to-one. The rule-based stages, such as hypothesis generation and symbolic verification, are consistently strong across all models, whereas the largest differences emerge during evidence-guided refinement and final diagnosis, where the model must use visual evidence to revise and apply its hypotheses. Hence weaker models can maintain relatively high agentic scores by successfully completing structured stages such as hypothesis generation and verification, while still struggling to translate that reasoning into the correct final classification. The resulting process–outcome gap highlights the value of ARISE’s stage-level evaluation, which reveals where reasoning quality stops carrying through to diagnostic performance.
