\section{Path to Deployment}
\begin{figure}
\centering
\includegraphics[width=1\linewidth]{figures/Deployment.png}
\caption{Illustration of an AI-enabled decision-support interface for explainable IBD assessment using intestinal ultrasound, designed to make model outputs accessible and interpretable to nurses.}
\label{fig:deployment}
\end{figure}
ARISE is designed as a clinician-in-the-loop decision-support system rather than an autonomous diagnostic tool, emphasizing interpretability, error detection, and compatibility with existing IBD imaging workflows. The current framework has been evaluated across two complementary IBD imaging settings: 3,498 wireless capsule endoscopy (WCE) images from the publicly available CrohnIPI dataset \cite{vallee2020crohnipi} and B-mode intestinal ultrasound data obtained from the C-TRUS dataset \cite{leenings2025ctrus} and annotated by an expert at the Indiana University (IU) School of Medicine. Clinical experts informed the visual cues, decision boundaries, and interpretation guidelines used by ARISE. Unlike black-box deep-learning based models, ARISE exposes the visual findings, diagnostic hypotheses, symbolic verification results, refinement steps, and final recommendation, allowing clinicians to inspect or override reasoning before acting on the output.
The next deployment stage is retrospective clinical validation using anonymized IU School of Medicine data from approximately 40 adults with Crohn's disease, collected by a gastrointestinal ultrasound expert certified by the International Bowel Ultrasound Group (IBUS), an international organization supporting standardized training, research, and clinical adoption of intestinal ultrasound for IBD care. Expert annotations of bowel-wall stratification and clinical assessments will serve as reference standards for evaluating ARISE predictions. Stage-level errors will be analyzed across disease severity, patient variation, image quality, and acquisition hardware.
For patients with anonymized, clinically indicated endoscopy data, ARISE will also estimate the Simple Endoscopic Score for Crohn's Disease (SES-CD) \cite{DAPERNO2004505}, from AI-identified ulcer size, ulcerated surface, and luminal narrowing across the terminal ileum and four colonic segments. This will be compared to the SES-CD score assessments by experts. 
Following retrospective validation, ARISE will be incorporated into a software-based clinical interface capable of processing endoscopy and B-mode ultrasound data streams. In an initial shadow-mode study, ARISE outputs will be compared with specialist interpretation without influencing patient care. A subsequent clinician-in-the-loop pilot will allow specialists to accept, question, or override intermediate reasoning and final recommendations, providing direct evidence of where the system's explanations are clinically useful or insufficient.
Finally, nurses and generalist clinicians will participate in a small case study evaluating whether ARISE's visual-cue explanations improve non-expert interpretation and diagnostic accuracy relative to unaided assessment (Figure \ref{fig:deployment}). Successful prospective evaluation would support testing ARISE across multiple hospitals and integrating it with existing clinical imaging and reporting systems. This staged progression, from retrospective validation, to silent real-time testing, to clinician-assisted evaluation and multi-site deployment, provides a practical path toward safe, interpretable use of ARISE in IBD assessment. 
\section{Conclusion}
We presented ARISE, a five-stage agentic framework that adapts a frozen vision-language model to few-shot IBD assessment by generating, grounding, verifying, and refining diagnostic hypotheses without any fine-tuning. Across two independent cohorts (WCE for Crohn's disease and B-mode ultrasound for ulcerative colitis) and three VLM families, ARISE improved accuracy, F1, precision, and recall over baselines on every model–task combination, with the largest gains on the hardest task and weakest baseline model, while its stage-level scoring exposed cases where strong intermediate reasoning did not fully carry through to final accuracy, highlighting a gap a single outcome metric would hide. Beyond raw accuracy, ARISE's key contribution is auditability: every reasoning stage is stored as an inspectable artifact rather than collapsed into an opaque prediction, directly addressing the domain-reasoning gap, hallucination risk, data scarcity, and black-box behavior limiting clinical VLM adoption. Future work extends this toward improving the model with the agentic scores and deployment in real-world clinical settings. 
