We organized our evaluation around the distinct types of information required from an echocardiogram. Frame-level experiments assessed acquisition context, localized cardiac geometry, anatomical boundaries, and age-related developmental information. Video-level experiments assessed cardiac function and structural heart disease (SHD) classification after temporal fusion. Comparisons followed a consistent hierarchy: EchoDino assessed baseline feature extraction, EchoDino-PATCH assessed localized spatial reaggregation, and EchoDino-MEMS assessed temporal evidence selection. To rigorously evaluate the learned representations, the foundation model backbone itself remained strictly frozen during downstream evaluation, with only the task-specific heads being trainable. Public external datasets were evaluated using their official partitions when available. For TCH cohorts lacking predefined partitions, we created patient-disjoint, label-stratified training, validation, and test sets; cohort-specific partition sizes and scanner-vendor coverage are reported in Supplementary Tables~\ref{si-tab:tch_view_distribution}--\ref{si-tab:tch_vendor_distribution}. Hyperparameters and model checkpoints were selected using validation performance only, and final performance was reported on the held-out test sets. Unless otherwise specified, performance is reported as bootstrap medians with percentile 95\
EchoDino was initialized from natural-image DINOv3 weights and subsequently adapted using only pediatric echocardiography videos. We therefore evaluated its transferability across five internal pediatric datasets, one external pediatric dataset, and three external adult datasets (Table~\ref{tab:tch_cohort_summary} and Sec.~\ref{sec:datasets}). Fig.~\ref{fig:EchoDino_arch}d summarizes performance across classification, measurement, segmentation, and video-prediction tasks. These results demonstrate that EchoDino generally outperforms the evaluated baseline encoders under a frozen-encoder protocol across pediatric and adult cohorts on diverse downstream tasks, including global (view classification, age prediction), localized (cardiac measurement, structural heart disease diagnosis), dense (chamber segmentation), and temporal endpoints (ejection-fraction regression, sweep-mode recognition). 
\subsection{EchoDino captures global acquisition context and localized cardiac anatomy}
\textbf{EchoDino captures the global organization of echocardiographic images.} View classification tests whether a frame representation contains sufficient acquisition context to distinguish clinically recognized echocardiographic acquisitions. On the TCH-View dataset, this task comprised 52 labels that jointly encoded acquisition mode (e.g., two-dimensional imaging or color Doppler), standard view family, and a reference designation, anatomical level, or focus (e.g., a parasternal long-axis reference view or a parasternal short-axis view at the mitral-valve/papillary-muscle level). We evaluated the frozen representation using K-nearest-neighbor  (KNN) classification and a linear probe. EchoDino achieved an accuracy of 0.850 at k = 10 and a linear-probe accuracy of 0.889 (Table~\ref{tab:view_classification}). By comparison, PanEcho achieved 0.504 and 0.609, whereas DINOv3 achieved 0.369 and 0.464, respectively. All comparisons with EchoDino were highly significant (p $<$ 0.001), and its k-nearest-neighbor accuracy remained robust at 0.848 for both k = 20 and k = 30. Detailed per-class results are provided in Supplementary Note~\ref{si-note:view-classification} and Supplementary Table~\ref{si-tab:view_classification_per_class}. This task-head-free performance establishes that EchoDino’s frozen representation directly and accurately encodes global echocardiographic acquisition context.
