\section{Discussion}
The central finding of this study is that a single, strictly frozen self-supervised representation adapted to pediatric echocardiography can seamlessly support a broad spectrum of visual, anatomical, and functional tasks. Conventionally, automated echocardiographic analysis has relied on fragmented, task-specific models or massive annotated datasets that constrain feature expressivity and scale poorly across heterogeneous clinical workflows. Task-specific models manage this by narrowing the feature space, which in turn requires a large and relatively homogeneous labeled cohort for each task. Cohorts of that size and uniformity are uncommon in pediatric cardiology, and most published models have consequently remained at proof of concept. EchoDino shifts the burden to the representation. By continually adapting DINOv3 via self-supervised pretraining on 3.7 million unlabeled pediatric frames, the network reorganizes its feature space around recurring cardiac geometry and kinetic patterns without relying on downstream diagnostic labels. Consequently, across nine pediatric and adult datasets, this shared representation supported global view recognition, localized measurement, dense segmentation, developmental age estimation, study-level structural heart disease (SHD) classification, and ejection fraction (EF) prediction through lightweight task-specific readouts. These results establish that a unified visual foundation can retain the fine-grained spatial and temporal evidence required for multi-task echocardiographic analysis, eliminating the need to retrain or redesign the core image encoder for each downstream application.
The frame-level experiments clarify how downstream performance depends on matching readout architectures to the spatial organization of the frozen tokens. High view-classification accuracy via $k$-nearest-neighbor and linear probing confirms that global acquisition context is readily accessible from the class token. However, relying solely on global readouts compresses critical spatial detail. By introducing EchoDino-PATCH, we demonstrated that spatially resolved patch tokens natively preserve fine-grained local geometry. Accessing these patch tokens significantly reduced errors in cardiac measurement and age estimation, while intermediate feature hierarchies enabled precise chamber and myocardial boundary segmentation. Crucially, this confirms that EchoDino's frozen tokens function as an expressive visual substrate: local anatomical detail is intrinsically preserved, but harvesting it requires spatial routing tailored to the geometric demands of the clinical target.
Our video-level evaluation demonstrates that dynamic temporal modeling can be achieved without making the foundational encoder intrinsically video-dependent. Independently encoded frame features provided a robust foundation for temporal reasoning, supporting EF regression via a shallow temporal module and study-level SHD diagnosis through prediction aggregation. Furthermore, by introducing MEMS, we addressed a fundamental flaw in conventional video pipelines: content-agnostic uniform frame sampling, which risks omitting brief cardiac phases while expending computational budget on visually redundant frames. MEMS corrects this by prioritizing temporally diverse, high-motion frames, consistently reducing EF prediction errors across evaluated cohorts. This motion-aware selection proved particularly advantageous in infants, where rapid heart rates make capturing brief end-systolic and end-diastolic extremes challenging. Conversely, for sweep-mode acquisitions where discriminative evidence is distributed uniformly across probe traversals, standard sampling remained effective, though MEMS still yielded incremental gains in classification area under the curve (AUC). Together, these findings validate content-aware frame selection as a general mechanism for efficient temporal reasoning in cardiac imaging.
Extending model evaluation to external pediatric and adult benchmarks underscores the broad generalizability of the learned features. Despite pretraining exclusively on pediatric frames, EchoDino's frozen representation transferred effectively to adult datasets, improving ventricular measurement on EchoNet-LVH, tissue segmentation on CAMUS, and EF estimation on EchoNet-Dynamic. This cross-domain success indicates that self-supervised pretraining captures fundamental principles of cardiac anatomy and physiology---such as recurring chamber geometry, tissue boundaries, and phase-dependent contraction mechanics---rather than memorizing cohort-specific demographic identifiers or institutional imaging artifacts. While these findings highlight the potential for tools built on a shared encoder to follow patients transitioning from pediatric to adult congenital heart disease (ACHD) care, direct validation in ACHD populations remains necessary to establish longitudinal measurement consistency as anatomy evolves with age and surgical intervention.
In pediatric cardiology, where marked anatomical heterogeneity, developmental growth, and scarce labeled datasets have long impeded artificial intelligence translation, a reusable foundation model offers a compelling framework. By absorbing developmental and structural variation during label-free pretraining, EchoDino provides a scalable substrate for developing modular clinical tools---ranging from automated acquisition sorting and study-completeness checks to preliminary chamber sizing and functional tracking---without the burden of retraining separate encoders. Crucially, however, the primary value of such automated tools lies in supporting, rather than replacing, expert clinical judgment. Diagnostic evaluation in congenital heart disease is intrinsically integrative, requiring specialists to synthesize spatial relationships, ventricular morphology, loading conditions, surgical history, and complex hemodynamics. Automated extraction of anatomical and functional targets can nonetheless provide a reliable quantitative baseline, reducing repetitive manual measurements, improving inter-examination standardization, and enhancing efficiency during longitudinal surveillance.
Several limitations outline key priorities for future research. First, the primary TCH-SHD endpoint was evaluated as a binary composite classifier combining diverse congenital lesions, valvar regurgitations, and stenoses. Although phenotype-stratified recall analyses indicate broad coverage across underlying conditions, binary detection is fundamentally distinct from detailed anatomical characterization. The current model was not evaluated on sequential segmental analysis, lesion-specific variant discrimination, associated anomalies, or residual postoperative defects. Developing readouts capable of predicting these intermediate anatomical details represents a necessary step toward clinical utility and model explainability.
Second, aggregate performance metrics---such as mean absolute error, Dice coefficients, AUC, and classification accuracy---summarize overall population trends but do not guarantee individual point-of-care actionability. Clinical implementation demands rigorous characterization of measurement bias, limits of agreement, threshold-specific performance, and outlier error rates. This is especially vital in high-risk clinical subgroups, including patients with severe ventricular dysfunction, systemic right or single-ventricle physiology, complex postoperative anatomy, or poor acoustic windows. Finally, for automated tools to safely support clinical decision-making, future iterations must incorporate robust uncertainty estimation and automated quality-control mechanisms capable of flagging degraded or ambiguous examinations where model outputs may be unreliable.
\section{Methods}
To decouple the foundation encoder from individual downstream tasks and assess its transferability across applications, we adapted the self-supervised DINOv3 framework to echocardiography and developed EchoDino (Fig.~\ref{fig:EchoDino_arch}). Self-supervised pretraining does not optimize the encoder for a single clinical label and can produce general-purpose global and localized image representations~\citep{DinoV3}. 
EchoDino is a frame-based foundation model with a ViT~\citep{Vit} backbone. Its frozen encoder provides three complementary representations. The normalized class token summarizes global image context, whereas the grid of normalized final-layer patch tokens retains spatially localized information. Intermediate features extracted across multiple encoder depths preserve fine boundary detail and higher-level anatomy for dense prediction. Together, these representations support static anatomical tasks through lightweight readouts and cardiac-dynamics tasks through shallow temporal fusion, while the backbone remains frozen. In contrast, applying a video foundation model to still-image tasks commonly requires repeating a static frame to construct a pseudo-video~\citep{v-jepa}, an artificial input construction that EchoDino avoids.
\subsection{Domain adaptation of a DINOv3 foundation encoder}
DINOv3 is an image-only self-supervised framework for pretraining vision transformers without text captions or task labels~\citep{DinoV3}. It uses a teacher--student architecture in which the teacher’s predictions supervise the student. The teacher parameters are updated as an exponential moving average of the student parameters, and gradient back-propagation is limited to the student. DINOv3 concurrently learns from whole-image views and masked image patches. Across multiple crops of the same image, it aligns image-level representations to capture global context despite changes in framing, scale, and visible content. The student also predicts teacher features for masked patches, retaining localized and spatially resolved information. During extended training, a dense-feature preservation signal maintains pairwise relationships among patch tokens, supporting spatially coherent representations.
We selected DINOv3 because echocardiographic analysis requires both global and localized representations. View recognition depends on the overall cardiac field of view and acquisition context, whereas segmentation and measurement require spatially coherent features for chambers, ventricular walls, valves, and other anatomical structures. Its multi-crop image-level objective is therefore suited to variation in probe framing and magnification, while masked patch prediction and Gram anchoring support dense anatomical representation learning. EchoDino was initialized from the released DINOv3 ViT-L/16 checkpoint pretrained on LVD-1689M~\cite{DinoV3} and subsequently adapted to 3,747,848 unlabeled pediatric echocardiography frames sampled from 1,681,739 videos.
Although we retained the DINOv3 teacher--student framework, we adapted data construction and augmentation to echocardiographic acquisitions. The student received two $256\times256$ global crops covering 50--100\
\subsection{Frame-level downstream evaluation}
\label{sec: frame-level method}
Frame-level echocardiography tasks require representations at different spatial scales. View recognition depends primarily on the complete acquisition context, whereas cardiac measurement and segmentation require localized anatomical information. We therefore evaluated three readouts from the frozen EchoDino encoder: the final class token, reaggregated final-block patch tokens, and intermediate multiscale patch features.
\textbf{The class-token readout measures task-relevant information directly accessible from EchoDino's global representation.} Each frame was represented by its normalized final class token. For parametric classification and regression, a task-specific linear head mapped this token to the prediction target. View recognition was additionally evaluated using k-nearest neighbors without fitting a parametric head. This class-token readout therefore evaluates global representation quality without task-specific spatial reaggregation or backbone optimization.
\textbf{EchoDino-PATCH reaggregates localized cardiac anatomy when a single global token is insufficient.} The readout concatenated the normalized class token with 256 normalized final-block patch tokens. A four-block, eight-head self-attention decoder allowed the class token to aggregate evidence across the complete $16\times16$ patch grid. Two-dimensional rotary positional encoding preserved patch rows and columns, allowing attention to distinguish anatomically different locations. Final layer normalization and a linear regression head mapped the updated class token to the measurement target. Only the decoder and regression head were optimized. EchoDino-PATCH is therefore a lightweight nonlinear readout, and its comparison with the class-token readout isolates the value of explicit patch access.
\textbf{Intermediate feature hierarchies preserve both boundary detail and high-level anatomy for dense segmentation.} A final-block token grid may omit fine information retained at earlier network depths. Following the DINOv3 linear segmentation protocol, we extracted normalized features from zero-indexed EchoDino transformer blocks $[4,11,17,23]$. The four feature maps were spatially aligned and concatenated along the channel dimension. The segmentation head applied spatial dropout, synchronized batch normalization, and a $1\times1$ convolution to produce patch-grid logits. Bilinear interpolation restored the logits to the input resolution, while the encoder remained frozen. For the EchoPrime baseline, following V-JEPA~\citep{v-jepa}, a single frame was repeated across 16 temporal positions required by its MViTv2~\citep{mVit} backbone. We temporally averaged features from the final transformer blocks of its four resolution stages, at zero-indexed block indices $[0,2,13,15]$. For the PanEcho ConvNeXt~\citep{convnext} backbone, we extracted the four feature-stage outputs at indices $[1,3,5,7]$. These choices yield one output from each resolution stage defined by the hierarchical MViTv2 and ConvNeXt architectures.
\subsection{Video-based temporal fusion}
Video-level echocardiography tasks require spatial evidence from individual frames and temporal evidence across cardiac motion. EchoDino separates these components so that static tasks do not require a video backbone. The frozen frame encoder provides spatial representations, while a lightweight temporal module learns task-specific video aggregation.
\textbf{EchoDino extends frozen frame representations to video prediction through a shallow temporal readout.} Each video was represented by $K=16$ sampled frames. EchoDino independently encoded each frame as one 1,024-dimensional class token and 256 patch tokens. The temporal module averaged the frame-level class tokens to initialize one global sequence token. It then discarded the individual class tokens and flattened all patch tokens across frames. A four-block, eight-head transformer updated the global token by aggregating localized evidence across anatomy and time. Only the temporal module and final regression or classification head were optimized.
\textbf{Multimodal Rotary Positional Embedding (M-RoPE) preserves cardiac location and irregular temporal spacing after frame selection.} Flattening the patch tokens removes their spatial and temporal coordinates. We therefore applied M-RoPE~\cite{mrope} to the attention queries and keys using separate rotary dimensions for patch row, patch column, and source-frame position. For sampled source indices $s_1,\ldots,s_K$, the normalized temporal coordinate was
\begin{equation}
\tau_k=(K-1)\frac{s_k-\min_j s_j}{\max_j s_j-\min_j s_j+\epsilon}.
\end{equation}
We set $\epsilon=10^{-6}$ to avoid division by zero. This transformation maps each video to a common range while retaining relative gaps between nonuniformly selected frames. After four fusion blocks and final layer normalization, the global token was passed to the task head.
\subsection{Motion-biased Entropy Maximization Sampling (MEMS)}
\label{sec:mems}
Uniform sampling provides temporal coverage but may repeatedly select similar cardiac phases or prolonged static intervals. Diversity sampling reduces visual redundancy but can concentrate selections within a temporally narrow portion of a video. MEMS combines temporal anchors, feature-space coverage, and a motion bias to balance these limitations (Fig.~\ref{fig:EchoDino_arch}c,g).
\textbf{Temporal anchors guarantee coarse coverage across the complete echocardiography video.} Given $V=\{v_i\}_{i=0}^{N-1}$ with $N\geq K\geq C$, MEMS first selects $C$ uniformly distributed anchors and samples $K$ frames. The index $c_j$ of anchor $j$ is
\begin{equation}
c_j = j\left\lfloor\frac{N}{C}\right\rfloor + \left\lfloor\frac{N}{2C}\right\rfloor,
\end{equation}
and the seed set is
\begin{equation}
S_{\mathrm{seed}} = \{ v_{c_j} \mid j = 0, 1, \dots, C-1 \}.
\end{equation}
If $C=K$, MEMS reduces to uniform sampling.
\textbf{Frozen EchoDino features quantify frame diversity and cardiac motion without requiring downstream labels.} For each frame $v_i$, we compute the L2-normalized class-token embedding
\begin{equation}
x_i = \frac{\phi(v_i)}{\|\phi(v_i)\|_2}.
\end{equation}
Feature-space motion is the distance between adjacent embeddings. The first observed change is assigned to both initial positions so that the score vector contains $N$ elements:
\begin{equation}
\widetilde{m}_i = \|x_i - x_{i-1}\|_2 \quad (i=1,\ldots,N-1),
\qquad \widetilde{m}_0=\widetilde{m}_1.
\end{equation}
We then normalize the motion scores,
\begin{equation}
m_i = \frac{\widetilde{m}_i}{\mu_m+\epsilon},
\qquad
\mu_m = \frac{1}{N}\sum_{j=0}^{N-1}\widetilde{m}_j,
\end{equation}
where $\epsilon=10^{-6}$ provides numerical stability. Mean normalization makes the motion weight less sensitive to the overall magnitude of feature changes. Frame entropy is measured by cosine distance:
\begin{equation}
d(v_a, v_b) = 1 - (x_a \cdot x_b).
\end{equation}
\textbf{Motion-biased farthest-point sampling favors distinct cardiac appearances near larger motion transitions.} Starting from $S=S_{\mathrm{seed}}$, MEMS iteratively selects
\begin{equation}
v_i^* = \arg\max_{v_i \in V \setminus S}
\left[
\left(\min_{v_k \in S} d(v_i, v_k)\right)
\times
(1 + \lambda m_i)
\right],
\end{equation}
where $\lambda$ controls the motion bias. The minimum-distance term greedily expands feature-space coverage and serves as an entropy-maximization surrogate. Multiplication by $(1+\lambda m_i)$ prioritizes frames near larger transitions without removing the diversity constraint. After each selection, the new frame is added to $S$, and all minimum distances are updated. Selection stops when $|S|=K$. The selected indices are sorted chronologically before temporal fusion to preserve acquisition order. Experimentally, we used $K=16$, $C=4$, and $\lambda=0.6$, producing four temporal anchors and twelve motion-biased diversity selections. The ablation study is provided in Supplementary Note~\ref{si-note:mems-ablation} and Supplementary Fig.~\ref{si-fig:EchoDynamic_ablation}.
\section{Experimental setup}
\subsection{Datasets}\label{sec:datasets}
The evaluation was designed to test representation quality across pediatric and adult populations, internal and external institutions, and tasks requiring global, localized, dense, or temporal information. Six datasets were derived from Texas Children's Hospital (TCH; Table~\ref{tab:tch_cohort_summary}). EchoNet-Pediatric provided an external pediatric benchmark, while CAMUS, EchoNet-LVH, and EchoNet-Dynamic provided adult benchmarks.
\textbf{TCH-Complex} provided the unlabeled pretraining corpus. The source archive contained 1,681,739 video records corresponding to 1,680,332 unique DICOM files from 17,984 studies and 13,323 pediatric patients. It included two-dimensional, three-dimensional, color Doppler, M-mode, B-mode, and related acquisitions. We sampled at most three frames from each video to limit repeated presentation of highly similar adjacent frames and prevent longer acquisitions from contributing disproportionately to pretraining. This procedure produced 3,747,848 pretraining images. 
\textbf{TCH downstream cohorts} were curated separately for their respective tasks, as shown in Table~\ref{tab:tch_cohort_summary}. \textbf{TCH-SHD} comprised 1,015,795 retained videos from 16,427 studies in 12,517 patients. At the patient level, 4,736 patients had cardiologist-provided labels for a broad class of significant structural heart disease (select congenital and acquired heart disease), and 7,781 were labeled normal. Twenty-four non-mutually-exclusive diagnostic and phenotypic labels were reserved for post hoc stratification. Detailed label definitions are provided in Supplementary Methods.
\textbf{TCH-View} contained 10,522 retained videos from 301 studies in 298 patients. The videos were assigned to 52 cardiologist-annotated classes that jointly encoded acquisition mode, standard view family, and a reference designation, anatomical level, or focus.
\textbf{TCH-EF} contained 13,809 apical four-chamber videos from 3,864 studies in 2,637 patients, paired with study-level EF. Mean EF was \(46.59\pm14.60\
\textbf{TCH-Age} retained the first frame from each eligible video and comprised 101,636 frames from 3,865 studies in 2,915 patients aged 0--18 years. The frames represented the 11 two-dimensional view categories reported in Supplementary Table~\ref{si-tab:tch_age_composition}. The parasternal categories were the short-axis view (\texttt{2D\_PSAX}) and long-axis view (\texttt{2D\_PLAX}). The apical categories were the four-chamber (\texttt{2D\_A4C}), two-chamber (\texttt{2D\_A2C}), five-chamber (\texttt{2D\_A5C}), and three-chamber (\texttt{2D\_A3C}) views. The subcostal categories were the long-axis view (\texttt{2D\_SCLAX}) and short-axis views acquired with the indicator at 12~o'clock (\texttt{2D\_SCSAX12}) or 6~o'clock (\texttt{2D\_SCSAX6}). The suprasternal-notch categories were the long-axis view (\texttt{2D\_SSNLAX}) and short-axis view (\texttt{2D\_SSNSAX}). For TCH-Age only, frame-level predictions were aggregated within each patient before evaluation.
\textbf{TCH-Sweep} contained 13,410 videos from 316 studies in 313 patients and distinguished four acquisition modes. The cohort included 5,582 two-dimensional, 6,209 color-Doppler, 723 two-dimensional-sweep, and 896 color-Doppler-sweep videos. Detailed class and target distributions are provided in Supplementary Methods and Supplementary Tables~\ref{si-tab:tch_view_distribution}--\ref{si-tab:tch_sweep_composition}.
TCH-SHD is a subset of the TCH-Complex dataset, consisting of 56.8\
\textbf{EchoNet-Pediatric} \citep{EchoPed} contains 7,643 videos from 4,467 examinations in 1,958 patients younger than 18 years. The dataset provides apical four-chamber and parasternal short-axis videos with LV endocardial tracings at end diastole and end systole and clinical EF calculated using the pediatric 5/6 area--length method. We used it for frame-level LV segmentation and video-level EF prediction.
\textbf{CAMUS} \citep{CAMUS} contains apical two- and four-chamber sequences from 500 adults examined at the University Hospital of Saint-Etienne. Cardiologist annotations identify end-diastolic and end-systolic frames and delineate the LV endocardium, LV epicardium, and left atrium. We used CAMUS for frame-level segmentation of the LV cavity, myocardium, and left atrium.
\textbf{EchoNet-LVH} \citep{echoNetLVH} contains 12,000 adult parasternal long-axis videos with an official split of 9,600/1,200/1,200 videos for training, validation, and testing. Caliper annotations identify the interventricular septum, LV internal dimension, and LV posterior wall. We evaluated LVID at end diastole (LVIDd) and end systole (LVIDs), posterior-wall thickness at end diastole (LVPWd), and septal thickness at end diastole (IVSd).
\textbf{EchoNet-Dynamic} \citep{EchoDyanmic} contains 10,030 labeled adult apical four-chamber videos with LV tracings and clinical measurements of EF, end-systolic volume, and end-diastolic volume. We used it for frame-level volume regression, frame-level LV segmentation, and video-level EF prediction.
We used official partitions for public datasets if available. For TCH cohorts without predefined partitions, we constructed patient-disjoint, label-stratified training, validation, and test sets; cohort-specific partition sizes are reported in Supplementary Tables~\ref{si-tab:tch_view_distribution}--\ref{si-tab:tch_sweep_composition}.
\subsection{Preprocessing, optimization, and compute}
\textbf{Image overlays and metadata exclusion.} For TCH-Complex and all downstream TCH cohorts, videos were provided to the model only as pixel data after masking the top image overlay bar. No DICOM header fields, tabular metadata, patient identifiers, timestamps, scanner or vendor information, acquisition descriptions, or free-text reports were supplied as model inputs. These data were used only where necessary to assemble cohorts, define task labels, link videos within studies and patients, and construct patient-disjoint splits; they were not used as predictive features, input embeddings, or frame-selection signals.
\textbf{EchoDino Pretraining} Each frame was converted to a three-channel image and normalized using the ImageNet mean $(0.485,0.456,0.406)$ and standard deviation $(0.229,0.224,0.225)$. The student received two $256\times256$ global crops covering 50--100\
\textbf{Downstream Tasks} Frames were resized without changing their aspect ratio and zero-padded to $256\times256$ for EchoDino or $224\times224$ for PanEcho and EchoPrime. Training images were randomly rotated by up to $\pm15^\circ$; validation and test images received no stochastic augmentation. EchoDino and PanEcho inputs were scaled to $[0,1]$ and normalized using ImageNet statistics. EchoPrime inputs retained the $[0,255]$ intensity scale and used its prescribed channel mean and standard deviation. For frame-level EchoPrime experiments, each image was repeated across the 16 temporal positions required by its video backbone. We optimized only the task-specific readout for 30 epochs using AdamW, weight decay of $10^{-3}$, a three-epoch linear warm-up followed by cosine learning-rate decay, bfloat16 mixed precision, and gradient clipping at a norm of 1.0. Learning rates were selected on the validation set from task-specific grids spanning $5\times10^{-5}$ to $10^{-3}$, with the same candidates applied to every encoder compared on a given task. Training used two NVIDIA A6000 GPU processes and a fixed random seed of 42. Per-device batches were typically 128 for frame-level EchoDino and PanEcho experiments and 32 for video-level experiments. EchoPrime used a batch size of 16 because its 16-frame input requires substantially more memory, including when a still frame is repeated for frame-level evaluation.
\textbf{Baseline models.} We compared EchoDino with DINOv3, PanEcho, and EchoPrime to represent natural-image self-supervision, supervised echocardiography pretraining, and video-native vision--language pretraining, respectively. DINOv3 provided the initialization-matched control for measuring the effect of self-supervised adaptation to pediatric echocardiography \citep{DinoV3}. PanEcho provided an echocardiography-specific frame encoder trained within a view-agnostic multitask system on more than one million videos \citep{PanEcho}. EchoPrime provided the principal video-native comparison through an mViT encoder trained on more than 12 million video--report pairs \citep{EchoPrime}.
\textbf{Evaluation Metrics} Classification metrics included accuracy, precision, recall, F1 score, and area under the receiver operating characteristic curve (AUC), where applicable. Regression metrics included mean absolute error (MAE) and mean squared error (MSE), and segmentation performance was measured using the Dice coefficient. We used 10,000 paired nonparametric bootstrap resamples to estimate median performance, percentile 95\
