\section*{Supplementary Methods}
\label{si-sec:supplementary-methods}
\subsection*{TCH-SHD label definition}
\label{si-method:tch-shd-definition}
The structural heart disease phenotype was defined using a prespecified, rule-based combination of structured congenital lesion variables, structured valvar regurgitation grades, and valve-stenosis severity extracted from the echocardiography report summary. The resulting SHD labels were reviewed by an expert cardiologist. An examination was classified as positive for structural heart disease if it demonstrated at least one congenital structural cardiac lesion, moderate or severe pulmonary, tricuspid, mitral, or aortic regurgitation, or moderate-or-greater pulmonary, aortic, mitral, or tricuspid stenosis.
Congenital structural heart disease was defined by the presence of at least one lesion-level diagnosis, including d-transposition of the great arteries, congenitally corrected transposition of the great arteries, coarctation of the aorta, interrupted aortic arch, truncus arteriosus, aortopulmonary window, total anomalous pulmonary venous connection, tetralogy of Fallot, double-outlet right ventricle, double-outlet left ventricle, complete or other atrioventricular septal defect, tricuspid atresia, pulmonary atresia, pulmonary atresia with intact ventricular septum, critical pulmonary stenosis, critical aortic stenosis, Ebstein anomaly, hypoplastic left heart syndrome, hypoplastic left ventricle, hypoplastic right ventricle, double-inlet left ventricle, double-inlet right ventricle, atrial septal defect, ventricular septal defect, or patent ductus arteriosus. Moderate or severe valvar regurgitation was identified from structured regurgitation grades. Moderate-or-greater valvar stenosis was identified using valve-specific extraction of stenosis severity from the echocardiography report summary. Examinations meeting none of the congenital lesion, clinically significant valvar regurgitation, or moderate-or-greater valvar stenosis criteria were classified as negative. Postoperative status alone did not determine the phenotype; repaired or palliated congenital heart disease remained classified as positive when the underlying congenital diagnosis was present.
