\section{Introduction}\label{sec:introduction}}
\IEEEPARstart{M}{edical} image segmentation is central to computer-assisted diagnosis {\cite{wang2024cardiovascular}}. The success of current deep learning-based techniques largely relies on massive, centralized datasets. However, clinical data are siloed across institutions, and strict data protection regulations make centralized training impractical. Federated learning (FL) {\cite{FedAvg_AISTATS17}} addresses this by letting multiple medical sites jointly optimize a global model through periodic parameter aggregation, without sharing raw data. Yet in clinical deployments, FL is hampered by data heterogeneity {\cite{FLSurveyandBenchmarkforGenRobFair_TPAMI24, zhao2024morestyle}}. Data from different sites are not independent and identically distributed (non-IID), mainly because of domain shifts caused by different scanners, acquisition protocols, and patient demographics {\cite{jiang2022harmofl, wang2025pfedsam, chen2024think}}. These feature discrepancies pull local models toward their own data distributions, which slows global convergence and degrades segmentation performance. As illustrated in Fig.~\ref{fig:motivation}, applying prototype-based FL to dense medical segmentation raises two client-side representation challenges that motivate our design.
\begin{figure}[t]
\centering
\includegraphics[width=\columnwidth]{figures/motivation.pdf}
\caption{\textbf{Two client-side representation challenges in prototype-based federated medical segmentation.}
\textbf{\mbox{Challenge~1}:} Single-layer alignment misses complementary multi-level
context, while prototypes dominated by region-center semantics underrepresent sparse
boundary structures. Coupling these cues in a single prototype further causes
cross-client misalignment.
\textbf{\mbox{Challenge~2}:} Site-specific style discrepancies
propagate across network layers, producing conflicting feature distributions and corrupting
the resulting prototypes. FedBCS+ addresses these issues with DCPA for decoupled
semantic--structural alignment and FSR for style-purified representation learning; the
purified prototypes subsequently guide S2PA aggregation.}
\label{fig:motivation}
\end{figure}
To mitigate data heterogeneity, many existing works regularize local training to improve generalization {\cite{MOON_CVPR21,FedProx_MLSys2020}}. Subsequent works introduce federated prototype learning {\cite{FedProto_AAAI22,FPL_CVPR23}}, and recent efforts further refine these representations via clustering client features {\cite{FedPLVM_NeurIPS24}} or employing Gaussian mixture models {\cite{FedGMKD_NeurIPS24}}. However, these prototype-based methods were designed for classification and face a representational limitation in dense medical image segmentation: \textbf{Incomplete Contextual Representation Learning}. Unlike image-level classification, precise medical segmentation requires modeling both regional pathological semantics (for lesion identification) and fine-grained anatomical structures (for boundary delineation) {\cite{chen2024think, li2025expert, wang2022medical}}. Current FL approaches, largely inheriting paradigms from classification tasks, typically construct prototypes solely from the deepest network bottlenecks. This deep-layer pooling compresses spatial dimensions, forcing both semantic and structural cues into a single, coupled representation. Aligning these coupled features across clients entangles internal semantics with boundary details and causes semantic--structural interference: pursuing a global semantic consensus tends to suppress the multi-level structural cues that dense segmentation requires {\cite{decouple_body_edge_eccv20, sung2024contextrast}}. This raises the first question: \textbf{(a) How to explicitly decouple the feature space to capture more comprehensive context?}
Beyond incomplete contextual representations, existing methods face another limitation spanning both local and server sides of the federated pipeline: \textbf{Layerwise Style and Aggregation Biases}. On the local client side, medical images exhibit significant feature heterogeneity due to diverse scanners and imaging protocols {\cite{jiang2023fair, liu2021feddg}}. Existing methods align final-layer representations {\cite{FPL_CVPR23}}, yet domain-specific style discrepancies remain pronounced in intermediate layers {\cite{huang2017arbitrary, xu2021fourier}}. This persistent layer-wise discrepancy degrades the quality of extracted prototypes before they are even uploaded. At the server, conventional parameter aggregation weights client models by their local sample counts~\cite{FedAvg_AISTATS17}, without using prototype relationships to characterize distribution shifts. Under data skews~\cite{li2022federated, FedNH_AAAI23}, sample counts alone do not capture how style discrepancies affect local representations or how client distributions relate to one another. The resulting aggregation favors dominant domains and compounds the bias introduced during local feature extraction. A reliable global consensus therefore requires aggregation weights that account for the relationships among the uploaded prototypes. Together, the local style corruption and the global aggregation bias raise the second question: \textbf{(b) How to extract style-purified prototypes locally and exploit their correlations globally to rectify the consensus bias?}
To address these challenges, we propose \textbf{FedBCS+}, a federated medical segmentation framework that integrates style recalibration, decoupled prototype alignment, and prototype-guided parameter aggregation. As illustrated in Fig.~\ref{fig:framework}, we first employ Frequency-domain Style Recalibration (FSR) to adaptively modulate the amplitude spectra of intermediate features while preserving their phase information. This suppresses site-specific appearance variations before prototype construction and yields \textit{style-purified features}. From these features, Decoupled Contextual Prototype Alignment (DCPA) integrates multiple encoder and decoder levels to capture both regional semantics and fine-grained anatomical structure. It constructs separate semantic and structural prototypes from region-center and boundary features and aligns the two types independently across clients. This decoupling reduces the interference caused by a shared prototype.
On the server, Style-purified Semantic Prototype Aggregation (S2PA) uses the purified semantic and structural prototypes to guide model aggregation. It measures each client's prototype divergence from the global consensus and converts this information into adaptive parameter aggregation weights. Clients whose representations are under-represented in the consensus receive greater weight, so their distributions are better reflected in the global model. The same prototypes thus serve both local alignment and server-side aggregation.
A preliminary version of this work, FedBCS, was published at AAAI 2026~\cite{FedBCS_AAAI26}. It introduced FSR and Context-aware Dual-level Prototype Alignment (CDPA), which combine style recalibration with hierarchical encoder--decoder feature fusion for federated segmentation. FedBCS+ retains FSR and the multi-level fusion design. The journal version extends FedBCS in four ways:
\begin{itemize}
\item \textbf{Decoupled prototype alignment.} We develop DCPA, extending CDPA from coupled class prototypes to separate semantic and structural prototypes. Independent alignment of the two types reduces the interference between regional semantics and boundary structure and keeps the multi-level feature fusion of CDPA.
\item \textbf{Prototype-guided parameter aggregation.} We introduce S2PA to incorporate representation information into server aggregation. S2PA converts semantic--structural prototype divergence from the global consensus into adaptive model aggregation weights, so that under-represented clients contribute more to the global model.
\item \textbf{Extended convergence analysis.} We extend the conference analysis to account for prototype-guided aggregation and decoupled alignment. The bound distinguishes the contributions of the aggregation-dependent second moment, the positive semantic--structural gradient interaction, and the prototype-anchor perturbation. This analysis identifies sufficient conditions under which FedBCS+ attains a tighter bound than FedBCS.
\item \begingroup
\interlinepenalty=10000
\textbf{Broader empirical validation.} We extend the evaluation from two to five heterogeneous benchmarks by adding BUS, Polyp, and 3D FeTS~2022, which cover ultrasound, colonoscopy, and volumetric MRI. We report five-fold cross-validation results on the four 2D benchmarks and further examine cross-site generalization, component contributions, training dynamics, and representation quality.
\par\endgroup
\end{itemize}
